<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-3-3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Graphing Parabolas</title>

<assemblage>
<p>In this Lesson we consider the effects of the parameters <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> on the graph of the basic parabola.  You could have students work on the Activities in groups, stopping to summarize after each part.</p></assemblage>

<activity><title>Graphing Practice I</title>
<p><ol marker="a.">
  <li><p>Complete the table of values, then sketch the graph on the grid with the basic parabola and label the coordinates of three points on the graph.</p>
          <aside><p>Students have trouble evaluating even these simple quadratic expressions. A common error, for example, is to enter <m>-4^2</m> into the calculator when evaluating <m>x^2</m> at <m>-4</m>.</p></aside>
  
  <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
    <stack><p>i. <m>~y=\frac{1}{2}x^2</m></p>
        <tabular top="minor" halign="center" right="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor">              
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>-2</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>2</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell><m>y</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{0000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{0000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{0000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{0000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{0000}</m></cell></row>
        </tabular>
    <image source="act-3-3-1.png"></image></stack>
    
    <stack><p>ii. <m>~y=3x^2</m></p>
        <tabular top="minor" halign="center" right="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor">              
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>-2</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>2</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell><m>y</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{0000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{0000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{0000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{0000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{0000}</m></cell></row>
        </tabular>
    <image source="act-3-3-2.png"></image></stack></sidebyside></li>
  
    <li><p>Compare each graph to the basic parabola, <m>y=x^2</m>. How is it different?</p>
            <aside><p>Summarize the results and direct students' attention to the appropriate page in the textbook.</p></aside></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<activity><title>Graphing Practice II</title>
<p><ol marker="a.">
  <li><p>Complete the table of values, then sketch the graph on the grid with the basic parabola and label the coordinates of three points on the graph.</p>
  <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
    <stack><p>i. <m>~y=x^2 - 9</m></p>
        <tabular top="minor" halign="center" right="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor">              
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>-4</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>-3</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>1</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell><m>y</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell></row>
          </tabular>
    <image source="act-3-3-3.png"></image>
    </stack>

    <stack><p>ii. <m>~y=9 - x^2</m></p>
        <tabular top="minor" halign="center" right="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor">              
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>-4</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>-3</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>1</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell><m>y</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell></row>
          </tabular>
    <image source="act-3-3-3.png"></image>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside></li>

    <li><p>Find the <m>x</m>-intercepts of each graph.</p>
    <p>Find the vertex of each graph.</p></li>

    <li><p>Compare each graph to the basic parabola, <m>y=x^2</m>. How is it different?</p>
              <aside><p>Note the symmetry of each graph and the location of the vertex. Again direct students' attention to the appropriate page in the textbook.</p></aside></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<activity><title>Graphing Practice III</title>
<p><ol marker="a.">
  <li><p>Complete the table of values, then sketch the graph on the grid with the basic parabola and label the coordinates of three points on the graph.</p>
  <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
    <stack><p>i. <m>~y=x^2 + 6x</m></p>
        <tabular top="minor" halign="center" right="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor">              
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>-6</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>-4</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>-3</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>-2</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>1</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell><m>y</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell></row>
        </tabular>
    <image source="act-3-3-3.png"></image>
  </stack>
  <stack><p>ii. <m>~y=2x^2 - 6x</m></p>
      <tabular top="minor" halign="center" right="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor">              
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\frac{3}{2}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>3</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>4</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell><m>y</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{000}</m></cell></row>
        </tabular>
    <image source="act-3-3-3.png"></image>
    </stack>
    </sidebyside></li>
    <li><p>Find the <m>x</m>-intercepts of each graph.</p>
                <aside><p>Help students find the <m>x</m>-intercepts, pointing out the use of factoring instead of extracting roots to solve the equations.</p>
                <p>Show the class how to find the <m>x</m>-coordinate of the vertex as the average of the <m>x</m>-intercepts. They will also need to be prompted on how to find the <m>y</m>-coordinate of the vertex.</p></aside>
    <p>Find the vertex of each graph.</p></li>
  
    <li><p>Compare each graph to the basic parabola, <m>y=x^2</m>. How is it different?</p></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<activity><title>Intercepts and Vertex</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><p>How do we find the <m>y</m>-intercept of a parabola from its equation?</p></li>
        <li><p>How do we find the <m>x</m>-intercepts of a parabola from its equation?</p></li>
        <li><p>Recall that to find the average of two numbers we add them up, and divide the result by 2.</p></li>
    </ol></p></li>

    <li><p><ol>
        <li><p>If the <m>x</m>-intercepts of a parabola are <m>x=p</m> and <m>x=q</m>, what is the <m>x</m>-coordinate of its vertex?</p></li>
        <li><p>If the <m>x</m>-intercepts of a parabola are <m>x=0</m> and <m>x=\dfrac{-b}{a}</m>, what is the <m>x</m>-coordinate of its vertex?</p></li>
        <li><p>How do we find the <m>y</m>-coordinate of the vertex from the equation?</p>
              <aside><p>This Activity leads students to the formula for the <m>x</m>-coordinate of the vertex. After deriving the formula, demonstrate that it works on the graphs in the prevous Activity.</p></aside></li>
    </ol></p></li>

    <li><p>Describe how each of the three parameters <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> affect the graph of the basic parabola.</p>
        <p><ol>
          <li><p><m>y=ax^2</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>y=x^2 + c</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>y=x^2 + bx</m></p></li>
      </ol></p></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<subsection><title>Check Your Understanding</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>What are the <m>x</m>-intercepts of the graph of <m>~y=x^2-16</m>?  What are the <m>x</m>-intercepts of the graph of <m>y=x^2-16x</m>?</p></li>
    <li><p>What is the axis of symmetry of the graph of <m>y=x^2-16</m>?  What is the axis of symmetry of the graph of <m>~y=x^2-16</m>?</p></li>
    <li><p>How is the graph of <m>~y=2x^2+5~</m> different from the basic parabola?  What are the coordinates of its vertex?</p></li>
    <li><p>Compare the graphs of <m>y=x^2-25</m> and <m>y=25-x^2</m>.</p></li>
</ol></p>
</subsection>

<subsection><title>Wrap Up</title>
<p>In this Lesson, we worked on the following skills and goals related to quadratic models:</p>
<p><ul>
    <li><p>Describe the effect of each of the parameters <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> on the graph of a parabola</p></li>
    <li><p>Find the <m>x</m>- and <m>y</m>-intercepts of a parabola</p></li> 
    <li><p>Find the vertex of a parabola</p></li>
    <li><p>Sketch the graph of a parabola</p></li>
</ul></p>
</subsection>

<subsection><title>Questions for Writing or Discussion</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>Describe what the parameter <m>a</m> tells you about the graph of <m>y=ax^2</m>.</p></li>
    <li><p>Explain why the <m>x</m>-coordinate of the vertex is the average of the <m>x</m>-intercepts of the graph.</p></li>
    <li><p>Describe the differences between the graphs of <m>y=x^2-4</m> and <m>y=x^2-4x</m>.</p></li>
    <li><p>Explain why the graph of <m>y=x^2-2</m> is shifted down 2 units compared to the basic parabola.</p></li>
</ol></p>

<assemblage><title>Concept Questions</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>Which point on a parabola always lies on the axis of symmetry?</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li>The <m>x</m>-intercept</li>    
        <li>The <m>y</m>-intercept</li>
        <li>The vertex</li>  
        <li>The origin</li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>The <m>x</m>-intercept of the graph of <m>~y=ax^2+bx+c~</m> is the same as the value of which parameter?</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>a</m></li> 
        <li><m>b</m></li>
        <li><m>c</m></li>   
        <li>None of these</li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>What does the value of <m>a</m> tell us about the graph of <m>~y=ax^2+bx+c~</m>? </p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li>The <m>y</m>-intercept</li>  
        <li>The number of <m>x</m>-intercepts</li>
        <li>The <m>x</m>-coordinate of the vertex</li> 
        <li>The width of the parabola</li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>Give the equation of a parabola that has no <m>x</m>-intercepts.</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>y=x^2</m></li>
        <li><m>y=x^2+4</m></li>
        <li><m>y=x^2-4</m></li>
        <li><m>y=x^2+x</m></li>
      </ol></p></li>
</ol></p></assemblage>
</subsection>

</section>